erdict**: the composition 0.000 IS a no-viable-code generation failure (120/120 without a complete program, 0/120 wrong-semantics — semantics are never reached), and it SURVIVES both matched controls: verbatim training frames (b) leave 120/120 without a complete program (complete-program rate 0.000) and pass@1 0.000, and at primitive-matched program length the new composites are 80/80 + 80/80 without a complete program under BOTH wordings (c), pass@1 0.000/0.000 — so neither wording frame nor program length explains the 0.000. IMPORTANT qualification from control (a): rewording MASTERED primitive programs with never-seen families ALSO collapses generation (298/300 without a complete program, complete-program rate 0.007 vs 1.000 native), so no-viable-code is this model's generic response to out-of-distribution prompts, and unseen composite structure is one such trigger — sufficient even when the sentence frame is verbatim from training (prompt length is ruled out: the seen-surface exposed control runs at comparable prompt length with complete-program rate 0.675). The book's structural-generalization conclusion therefore survives the matched controls as a GENERATION failure on unseen compositions (never a wrong-program/semantic failure: composite wrong-semantics counts are held-out 0, seen-surface 0, length-matched 0), but its mechanism is prompt-OD generation collapse, not failed composition attempts: the honest claim is that the model cannot generate code for unseen compositions (nor for seen programs under unseen wording), not that it composes incorrectly.

